SCOPE
-----

BSL-1.0 applies to material authored for this project: the binding generator,
the D and C++ runtime, the generated D bindings and generated C++ shims and
trampolines, the project tools (qmltc-d, uic-d, qrc-d, lupdate-d and the
others), and the original tests, examples, build logic and documentation.

It applies EXCEPT where a file's own SPDX-License-Identifier says otherwise, or
where THIRD-PARTY.md records different terms for a path. Those statements win
over this one.

WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO
--------------------------

It does not relicense Qt, Qt test data, libclang, DUB dependencies, or any
other third-party work. Each keeps its upstream license, and using this project
does not by itself satisfy those licenses.

In particular: the bindings this project generates are bindings TO Qt. Whoever
distributes an application built with them must comply with the terms of every
Qt module that application links, which for the open-source Qt distribution is
normally LGPLv3 and for a defined set of modules is GPL-only. docs/licensing.md
states the project's distribution policy and docs/distributing-qt.md is written
for application distributors.

GENERATED OUTPUT
----------------

For files this project generates, the copyright holder grants BSL-1.0 for the
generator-authored portions — templates, boilerplate and emitted structure. That
grant does not change the license or ownership of the user's input (specs,
headers, QML, .ui files), and it grants no rights in the third-party APIs the
output refers to. Each generated file carries this statement in its own header.

THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL PRESENT IN THIS REPOSITORY
-----------------------------------------------

See THIRD-PARTY.md for the inventory, and REUSE.toml for the machine-readable
per-path license expressions. Some of it is GPL-3.0-only and is used only by
tests; it is excluded from every installed and released artifact, and a build
gate enforces that exclusion rather than a promise in prose.
